.TH GRANT 10 "October 2026" "1.1" "Neverdark Manual"
.SH NAME
grant \- Attribute any region to a player
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B grant
.I player
.RI [ width
.RI [ here
|
.BI world " N"
|
.B cosmos
|
.IR x " y z w" ]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
Give a region to
.IR player ,
who rules it from then on. The region may be anything you rule: a district
inside a planet, a whole planet, a region containing many planets, or the
cosmos itself. This is the general form of what \fBplanet\fP does for one
world and one owner (yourself).
.PP
With one argument the grant is this place: the deepest region you own where
you are standing.
.B grant bob
.PP
hands Bob the room's region. With a width (a plen, 0\-64) the
grant is the width\-wide ancestor of the point:
.B grant bob 32
carves a district around here;
.B grant bob 16 world 5
is planet-sized around world 5;
.B grant bob 0
is the cosmos. The point defaults to \fBhere\fP; give explicit coordinates
or stand where you mean for a lived-in district, since a sub-world
.B world
point names the aligned-first sub-region.
.PP
The target must be a player (\fBme\fP allowed). You must rule the new region
or an ancestor of it, and it may be no coarser than your coarsest holding:
a planet ruler grants planet, districts and cells, never a super-planet or
the cosmos, while the cosmos ruler grants anything. Refusals name the ruler.
.PP
A re-grant keeps the region's module set, like a \fBplanet\fP reclaim; the
new ruler unloads what they do not want and loads their own code with
\fBloadmod\fP. The target is told, with the region header.
.PP
Delegation grants, it does not secede: ancestors keep ruling underneath,
deepest wins for naming. And bare
.B grant bob
as the cosmos ruler in unclaimed space hands over the cosmos itself \-
write the width out
.RB ( grant " bob 0" )
when that is what you mean.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR planet,release,target,here,planets,loadmod,chown (10)
.SH AUTHOR
quirinpa <quirinpa@tty.pt>
